1
00:00:00,710 --> 00:00:02,160
Artificial intelligence

2
00:00:02,200 --> 00:00:04,030
is bringing back the mammoth.

3
00:00:04,260 --> 00:00:05,580
A few winters from now,

4
00:00:05,620 --> 00:00:06,140
you're standing

5
00:00:06,180 --> 00:00:07,620
at a fence in the snow.

6
00:00:07,810 --> 00:00:09,530
There's a small hand in yours.

7
00:00:09,770 --> 00:00:10,930
And behind the fence,

8
00:00:10,970 --> 00:00:12,250
the animal from the poster

9
00:00:12,290 --> 00:00:13,760
above that child's bed...

10
00:00:13,870 --> 00:00:14,960
lifts its head.

11
00:00:15,210 --> 00:00:16,430
Red-brown fur.

12
00:00:16,490 --> 00:00:17,590
Small ears.

13
00:00:17,770 --> 00:00:19,550
Breath steaming in the cold.

14
00:00:21,290 --> 00:00:23,190
That animal doesn't exist yet.

15
00:00:23,340 --> 00:00:25,060
Between an elephant and a mammoth,

16
00:00:25,100 --> 00:00:27,060
there are about 1.4 million

17
00:00:27,100 --> 00:00:28,710
differences in their DNA.

18
00:00:28,960 --> 00:00:30,260
Which ones make a mammoth?

19
00:00:30,890 --> 00:00:32,870
No human can check them all.

20
00:00:33,080 --> 00:00:34,980
No lab can change them all.

21
00:00:35,320 --> 00:00:36,420
So the search for the few

22
00:00:36,460 --> 00:00:38,450
that matter went to a machine.

23
00:00:38,680 --> 00:00:39,970
And the first test animals

24
00:00:40,010 --> 00:00:41,260
are already alive.

25
00:00:41,600 --> 00:00:42,310
Mice...

26
00:00:42,350 --> 00:00:43,550
with long, woolly,

27
00:00:43,590 --> 00:00:45,120
mammoth-style fur.

28
00:00:46,380 --> 00:00:47,960
The mice were the rehearsal.

29
00:00:48,110 --> 00:00:50,150
The real job is an elephant.

30
00:00:50,340 --> 00:00:51,250
And the same tools

31
00:00:51,290 --> 00:00:52,460
will help decide which

32
00:00:52,500 --> 00:00:53,540
of today's animals

33
00:00:53,580 --> 00:00:55,560
that child still gets to see.

34
00:00:56,660 --> 00:00:58,080
The mice belong to a company

35
00:00:58,120 --> 00:01:00,190
in Dallas called Colossal.

36
00:01:00,610 --> 00:01:02,380
And its plan is not to thaw

37
00:01:02,420 --> 00:01:03,870
out a frozen mammoth.

38
00:01:04,410 --> 00:01:05,620
The plan is to take

39
00:01:05,660 --> 00:01:07,430
the mammoth's living cousin,

40
00:01:07,470 --> 00:01:08,880
the Asian elephant...

41
00:01:09,060 --> 00:01:11,790
and rewrite its DNA for the cold.

42
00:01:12,140 --> 00:01:13,190
And the machine's

43
00:01:13,230 --> 00:01:14,340
part in that plan?

44
00:01:14,850 --> 00:01:16,360
As reported this year,

45
00:01:16,400 --> 00:01:17,570
artificial intelligence

46
00:01:17,610 --> 00:01:19,190
helps comb through mammoth

47
00:01:19,230 --> 00:01:20,770
and elephant DNA...

48
00:01:20,850 --> 00:01:22,070
and flag the differences

49
00:01:22,110 --> 00:01:24,190
most likely to build a trait.

50
00:01:24,490 --> 00:01:25,510
The thick fur.

51
00:01:25,610 --> 00:01:26,800
The layer of fat.

52
00:01:26,880 --> 00:01:28,040
The small ears.

53
00:01:28,460 --> 00:01:30,550
It doesn't change a single gene.

54
00:01:30,740 --> 00:01:32,250
It writes the list.

55
00:01:32,870 --> 00:01:34,780
And it has plenty to read.

56
00:01:35,160 --> 00:01:36,650
Frozen ground kept mammoth

57
00:01:36,690 --> 00:01:38,800
DNA for thousands of years...

58
00:01:39,030 --> 00:01:40,630
and Colossal says it now holds

59
00:01:40,670 --> 00:01:42,870
about 100 mammoth genomes.

60
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:45,370
100 full sets of DNA.

61
00:01:45,540 --> 00:01:47,780
So why is there still no mammoth?

62
00:01:48,950 --> 00:01:49,970
Because reading

63
00:01:50,010 --> 00:01:51,680
was never the hard part.

64
00:01:51,950 --> 00:01:53,640
Knowing what it means is.

65
00:01:54,280 --> 00:01:56,990
Start the count at 1.4 million.

66
00:01:57,190 --> 00:01:58,860
Most of those differences probably

67
00:01:58,900 --> 00:02:00,830
change nothing you would ever see.

68
00:02:00,980 --> 00:02:02,130
Only some of them change

69
00:02:02,170 --> 00:02:03,940
what a gene actually builds.

70
00:02:04,250 --> 00:02:05,250
One study found those

71
00:02:05,290 --> 00:02:07,450
in about 1,600 genes.

72
00:02:07,490 --> 00:02:09,400
Still far too many to rebuild.

73
00:02:09,790 --> 00:02:10,710
This is where the machine's

74
00:02:10,750 --> 00:02:11,970
ranking comes in...

75
00:02:12,250 --> 00:02:13,810
and the count drops again.

76
00:02:14,070 --> 00:02:15,390
Colossal's own list,

77
00:02:15,430 --> 00:02:16,750
by the latest reports?

78
00:02:16,790 --> 00:02:18,780
About 150 genes...

79
00:02:18,820 --> 00:02:19,820
and rising.

80
00:02:20,350 --> 00:02:21,300
And tested in a

81
00:02:21,340 --> 00:02:22,880
living animal so far?

82
00:02:23,170 --> 00:02:24,530
Only in those mice.

83
00:02:24,600 --> 00:02:27,210
Seven genes, changed at once.

84
00:02:27,410 --> 00:02:29,170
Critics add a caution.

85
00:02:29,400 --> 00:02:30,940
Genes like these were already

86
00:02:30,980 --> 00:02:32,990
known to change a mouse's coat.

87
00:02:33,260 --> 00:02:35,060
So the mice prove the editing...

88
00:02:35,110 --> 00:02:36,210
not the list.

89
00:02:36,510 --> 00:02:38,380
From seven genes in a mouse...

90
00:02:38,420 --> 00:02:41,120
to about 150 in an elephant.

91
00:02:41,300 --> 00:02:42,230
And the man who runs

92
00:02:42,270 --> 00:02:43,850
Colossal, Ben Lamm,

93
00:02:43,890 --> 00:02:44,850
has every reason

94
00:02:44,890 --> 00:02:46,090
to promise that a machine

95
00:02:46,130 --> 00:02:48,310
will close that gap overnight.

96
00:02:48,640 --> 00:02:49,640
He doesn't.

97
00:02:49,960 --> 00:02:50,757
[Ben Lamm] The foundation models

98
00:02:50,797 --> 00:02:51,873
and AI models aren't

99
00:02:51,913 --> 00:02:52,989
quite looking at the

100
00:02:53,029 --> 00:02:54,105
entire tree of life,

101
00:02:54,145 --> 00:02:55,222
and they're not going

102
00:02:55,262 --> 00:02:56,059
to magically overnight

103
00:02:56,099 --> 00:02:57,175
give us a genome

104
00:02:57,215 --> 00:02:58,860
and give us an answer.

105
00:02:59,560 --> 00:03:00,590
No magic.

106
00:03:00,770 --> 00:03:02,130
Nothing overnight.

107
00:03:02,270 --> 00:03:02,940
The machine doesn't

108
00:03:02,980 --> 00:03:04,440
hand over a mammoth.

109
00:03:04,670 --> 00:03:06,460
It hands over a short list...

110
00:03:06,500 --> 00:03:08,300
and somebody has to test it.

111
00:03:08,560 --> 00:03:10,030
But how would a machine even

112
00:03:10,070 --> 00:03:11,860
know which differences matter?

113
00:03:12,170 --> 00:03:13,620
For the mammoth, Colossal has

114
00:03:13,660 --> 00:03:15,540
not shown that step in public.

115
00:03:15,820 --> 00:03:17,450
This October, it did...

116
00:03:17,550 --> 00:03:18,750
for a different animal.

117
00:03:18,890 --> 00:03:19,780
A bird.

118
00:03:19,820 --> 00:03:20,820
The dodo.

119
00:03:21,830 --> 00:03:23,990
And the dodo is the harder case.

120
00:03:24,140 --> 00:03:25,170
For the mammoth, there

121
00:03:25,210 --> 00:03:26,970
are about 100 genomes.

122
00:03:27,170 --> 00:03:28,130
For the dodo,

123
00:03:28,170 --> 00:03:29,720
there is exactly one.

124
00:03:29,860 --> 00:03:30,960
One bird.

125
00:03:31,020 --> 00:03:32,490
Nothing to compare it with.

126
00:03:32,760 --> 00:03:34,750
So where does a machine begin?

127
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:36,010
With the living.

128
00:03:36,210 --> 00:03:37,670
Colossal says its model,

129
00:03:37,710 --> 00:03:38,910
called GenoMorph,

130
00:03:38,950 --> 00:03:40,260
studied more than 70

131
00:03:40,300 --> 00:03:41,820
living bird species...

132
00:03:41,870 --> 00:03:43,460
their DNA, and the exact

133
00:03:43,500 --> 00:03:44,910
shape of their skulls.

134
00:03:45,120 --> 00:03:46,380
That is how it learned which

135
00:03:46,420 --> 00:03:48,790
switches in the DNA shape a beak.

136
00:03:49,330 --> 00:03:51,340
Then it turned to the dodo...

137
00:03:51,490 --> 00:03:52,640
and came back with about

138
00:03:52,680 --> 00:03:55,110
2,300 candidates.

139
00:03:55,300 --> 00:03:58,910
2,300 places in the DNA that might

140
00:03:58,950 --> 00:04:00,800
have shaped that famous beak.

141
00:04:01,180 --> 00:04:02,180
Might.

142
00:04:02,460 --> 00:04:03,880
Colossal itself says

143
00:04:03,920 --> 00:04:05,390
this is not proof.

144
00:04:05,610 --> 00:04:07,050
Every candidate still has

145
00:04:07,090 --> 00:04:08,830
to prove itself in the lab.

146
00:04:09,110 --> 00:04:11,170
So that is how a machine finds

147
00:04:11,210 --> 00:04:12,780
the differences that matter.

148
00:04:13,040 --> 00:04:14,230
It doesn't know them.

149
00:04:14,350 --> 00:04:15,530
It learns them...

150
00:04:15,570 --> 00:04:17,630
from animals that are still alive.

151
00:04:18,780 --> 00:04:21,120
Today, the machine writes a list.

152
00:04:21,430 --> 00:04:22,510
The machines that follow

153
00:04:22,550 --> 00:04:24,410
will be asked for far more.

154
00:04:24,720 --> 00:04:25,700
This channel exists

155
00:04:25,740 --> 00:04:26,940
for one reason:

156
00:04:27,050 --> 00:04:27,870
so that the arrival

157
00:04:27,910 --> 00:04:29,010
of superintelligence

158
00:04:29,050 --> 00:04:30,550
goes well for everybody.

159
00:04:30,790 --> 00:04:31,790
Want that too?

160
00:04:31,830 --> 00:04:32,830
Then subscribe.

161
00:04:34,010 --> 00:04:35,820
A list is not an animal,

162
00:04:35,860 --> 00:04:36,860
of course.

163
00:04:37,020 --> 00:04:38,910
Somebody still has to grow one...

164
00:04:39,110 --> 00:04:40,990
and a bird grows in an egg.

165
00:04:41,260 --> 00:04:43,150
This spring, the company says,

166
00:04:43,220 --> 00:04:44,940
live chicks hatched from an egg

167
00:04:44,980 --> 00:04:46,650
no bird ever laid...

168
00:04:46,740 --> 00:04:48,090
a see-through skin,

169
00:04:48,130 --> 00:04:50,250
held in a 3D-printed shell.

170
00:04:51,670 --> 00:04:53,890
So suppose all of it works.

171
00:04:53,930 --> 00:04:55,230
Every pick, right.

172
00:04:55,450 --> 00:04:57,000
Is the animal that walks out...

173
00:04:57,040 --> 00:04:58,040
a mammoth?

174
00:04:58,980 --> 00:05:00,370
There is a way to check.

175
00:05:00,740 --> 00:05:02,410
Three animals made by editing

176
00:05:02,450 --> 00:05:03,400
a living relative

177
00:05:03,440 --> 00:05:04,860
are already alive.

178
00:05:05,110 --> 00:05:07,600
Romulus, Remus and Khaleesi.

179
00:05:08,040 --> 00:05:08,960
Colossal presented

180
00:05:09,000 --> 00:05:10,460
them as dire wolves...

181
00:05:10,500 --> 00:05:11,400
a wolf that died out

182
00:05:11,440 --> 00:05:12,990
thousands of years ago.

183
00:05:13,320 --> 00:05:15,450
They began as gray wolf cells.

184
00:05:15,600 --> 00:05:17,150
20 edits went in...

185
00:05:17,190 --> 00:05:18,980
guided by DNA from a tooth

186
00:05:19,020 --> 00:05:20,960
13,000 years old.

187
00:05:21,330 --> 00:05:22,630
Colossal says the oldest

188
00:05:22,670 --> 00:05:23,940
are now two years old,

189
00:05:23,980 --> 00:05:25,980
healthy, and hunting as a pack.

190
00:05:27,710 --> 00:05:29,960
So are they dire wolves?

191
00:05:32,820 --> 00:05:34,600
Wolf experts at the union behind

192
00:05:34,640 --> 00:05:35,660
the Red List of threatened

193
00:05:35,700 --> 00:05:37,390
species say no.

194
00:05:37,640 --> 00:05:38,600
And Colossal's own

195
00:05:38,640 --> 00:05:39,620
chief scientist,

196
00:05:39,660 --> 00:05:41,080
Beth Shapiro, has said

197
00:05:41,120 --> 00:05:42,440
plainly what they are.

198
00:05:42,880 --> 00:05:44,550
"Our animals are gray wolves

199
00:05:44,590 --> 00:05:46,780
with 20 edits that are cloned."

200
00:05:47,120 --> 00:05:48,360
That is what "bringing

201
00:05:48,400 --> 00:05:49,770
back" means today.

202
00:05:50,030 --> 00:05:51,420
The traits come back.

203
00:05:51,550 --> 00:05:53,000
The species does not.

204
00:05:53,350 --> 00:05:54,310
And it tells you what would

205
00:05:54,350 --> 00:05:56,380
really stand behind that fence.

206
00:05:56,600 --> 00:05:57,810
An Asian elephant...

207
00:05:57,850 --> 00:05:59,110
with a mammoth's fur,

208
00:05:59,150 --> 00:06:00,890
fat, and small ears.

209
00:06:01,200 --> 00:06:03,080
An elephant built for the cold.

210
00:06:03,660 --> 00:06:04,940
And not soon.

211
00:06:05,180 --> 00:06:06,460
The first calf was once

212
00:06:06,500 --> 00:06:08,490
promised for 2028.

213
00:06:08,750 --> 00:06:10,030
Now Colossal says

214
00:06:10,070 --> 00:06:11,970
the early 2030s...

215
00:06:12,350 --> 00:06:13,520
and its chief executive

216
00:06:13,560 --> 00:06:15,890
says there is no hard date.

217
00:06:18,920 --> 00:06:21,500
That is a hope, not a timetable.

218
00:06:21,890 --> 00:06:23,710
So far, nobody has reported

219
00:06:23,750 --> 00:06:25,090
a mammoth embryo...

220
00:06:25,290 --> 00:06:26,860
or a pregnant elephant.

221
00:06:27,130 --> 00:06:28,320
And an elephant carries her

222
00:06:28,360 --> 00:06:30,480
calf for almost two years.

223
00:06:30,610 --> 00:06:32,890
So is one shaggy elephant,

224
00:06:32,930 --> 00:06:34,350
years behind schedule,

225
00:06:34,390 --> 00:06:36,260
really what all of this is for?

226
00:06:37,520 --> 00:06:38,520
It isn't.

227
00:06:38,700 --> 00:06:40,490
The bigger prize is the animals

228
00:06:40,530 --> 00:06:41,710
that are still here.

229
00:06:41,920 --> 00:06:44,140
And the mammoth itself shows why.

230
00:06:44,460 --> 00:06:45,700
The last mammoths died

231
00:06:45,740 --> 00:06:47,980
only about 4,000 years ago,

232
00:06:48,020 --> 00:06:50,040
on a remote island in the Arctic.

233
00:06:50,290 --> 00:06:51,720
The great pyramids of Egypt

234
00:06:51,760 --> 00:06:53,220
were already standing.

235
00:06:53,410 --> 00:06:55,010
But nobody was there to freeze

236
00:06:55,050 --> 00:06:56,740
a single living cell.

237
00:06:56,980 --> 00:06:59,520
The ice kept only broken DNA.

238
00:07:00,330 --> 00:07:01,580
That is why every trait

239
00:07:01,620 --> 00:07:02,980
has to be rebuilt...

240
00:07:03,140 --> 00:07:04,920
difference by difference.

241
00:07:05,560 --> 00:07:06,650
Now take an American

242
00:07:06,690 --> 00:07:08,320
animal that got luckier.

243
00:07:08,500 --> 00:07:09,610
In the 1980s,

244
00:07:09,650 --> 00:07:11,420
somebody froze living cells

245
00:07:11,460 --> 00:07:12,340
from a black-footed

246
00:07:12,380 --> 00:07:13,910
ferret named Willa.

247
00:07:14,200 --> 00:07:15,190
Decades later,

248
00:07:15,230 --> 00:07:17,620
those cells became living clones.

249
00:07:17,780 --> 00:07:18,530
According to U.S.

250
00:07:18,570 --> 00:07:19,630
wildlife officials,

251
00:07:19,670 --> 00:07:21,030
one of them had babies

252
00:07:21,070 --> 00:07:22,160
two years ago...

253
00:07:22,290 --> 00:07:23,790
and last year, her babies

254
00:07:23,830 --> 00:07:25,170
had babies of their own.

255
00:07:25,410 --> 00:07:27,630
A ferret from the 1980s...

256
00:07:27,670 --> 00:07:28,970
has grandchildren.

257
00:07:29,250 --> 00:07:30,310
No short list.

258
00:07:30,350 --> 00:07:31,390
No guesswork.

259
00:07:31,430 --> 00:07:33,530
Just a freezer, opened in time.

260
00:07:35,360 --> 00:07:37,000
Now scale that up.

261
00:07:37,130 --> 00:07:38,850
The U.S. government and Colossal

262
00:07:38,890 --> 00:07:39,930
plan to freeze cells

263
00:07:39,970 --> 00:07:41,320
from every species

264
00:07:41,360 --> 00:07:43,130
on the country's endangered list.

265
00:07:43,350 --> 00:07:44,320
Every one.

266
00:07:44,360 --> 00:07:45,400
And the genomes are meant

267
00:07:45,440 --> 00:07:47,220
to go online, free.

268
00:07:47,520 --> 00:07:49,560
A freezer is not a forest.

269
00:07:49,620 --> 00:07:51,190
It protects no habitat.

270
00:07:51,440 --> 00:07:52,370
But think about what the

271
00:07:52,410 --> 00:07:53,740
machine learns from.

272
00:07:54,080 --> 00:07:55,060
The living.

273
00:07:55,100 --> 00:07:57,150
Every genome saved is one more

274
00:07:57,190 --> 00:07:58,040
lesson in which

275
00:07:58,080 --> 00:07:59,180
differences matter.

276
00:07:59,570 --> 00:08:00,290
Every species

277
00:08:00,330 --> 00:08:01,870
that vanishes unread...

278
00:08:01,930 --> 00:08:03,580
is a lesson gone for good.

279
00:08:04,170 --> 00:08:05,150
Even the mammoth

280
00:08:05,190 --> 00:08:06,450
depends on the living.

281
00:08:06,780 --> 00:08:07,710
Its mother would be

282
00:08:07,750 --> 00:08:09,150
an Asian elephant...

283
00:08:09,560 --> 00:08:11,890
and her species is endangered too.

284
00:08:12,220 --> 00:08:13,750
In North American zoos,

285
00:08:13,790 --> 00:08:14,700
the leading killer

286
00:08:14,740 --> 00:08:16,910
of her calves is a virus.

287
00:08:17,190 --> 00:08:19,200
This summer, researchers reported

288
00:08:19,240 --> 00:08:20,940
on a vaccine that Colossal

289
00:08:20,980 --> 00:08:22,170
helped pay for.

290
00:08:22,440 --> 00:08:23,450
Two young elephants who

291
00:08:23,490 --> 00:08:25,400
got it caught the virus...

292
00:08:25,570 --> 00:08:26,320
and came through

293
00:08:26,360 --> 00:08:27,600
without treatment.

294
00:08:27,840 --> 00:08:29,210
Two calves are a first

295
00:08:29,250 --> 00:08:30,970
sign, not proof.

296
00:08:31,370 --> 00:08:33,210
But the road to a mammoth begins

297
00:08:33,250 --> 00:08:35,200
with keeping elephants alive.

298
00:08:35,750 --> 00:08:37,830
And the elephant is not alone.

299
00:08:37,980 --> 00:08:39,700
This year's Red List counts about

300
00:08:39,740 --> 00:08:42,640
50,000 species as threatened.

301
00:08:43,820 --> 00:08:46,390
So here is what the evidence says.

302
00:08:46,780 --> 00:08:48,520
Artificial intelligence is helping

303
00:08:48,560 --> 00:08:49,200
to bring back

304
00:08:49,240 --> 00:08:50,680
the mammoth's traits...

305
00:08:50,770 --> 00:08:51,770
in an elephant.

306
00:08:51,970 --> 00:08:53,360
Not the mammoth itself.

307
00:08:53,580 --> 00:08:54,460
The mammoth draws the

308
00:08:54,500 --> 00:08:55,990
cameras and the money.

309
00:08:56,240 --> 00:08:57,600
The rescue itself happens

310
00:08:57,640 --> 00:08:58,920
somewhere quieter.

311
00:08:59,160 --> 00:09:00,120
In a freezer...

312
00:09:00,160 --> 00:09:01,780
and in a machine that is learning

313
00:09:01,820 --> 00:09:03,400
to read what's inside.

314
00:09:03,630 --> 00:09:05,920
So stand at that fence once more.

315
00:09:06,240 --> 00:09:08,410
Maybe, in the early 2030s,

316
00:09:08,450 --> 00:09:09,640
there really is a small

317
00:09:09,680 --> 00:09:10,810
hand in yours...

318
00:09:11,020 --> 00:09:12,560
and an elephant in a mammoth's

319
00:09:12,600 --> 00:09:14,120
coat lifts its head.

320
00:09:15,700 --> 00:09:16,670
Should it ever walk

321
00:09:16,710 --> 00:09:18,120
free on the tundra?

322
00:09:18,470 --> 00:09:19,850
Last year, the union

323
00:09:19,890 --> 00:09:21,380
behind the Red List voted

324
00:09:21,420 --> 00:09:22,880
on a pause for genetically

325
00:09:22,920 --> 00:09:24,780
modified wild animals.

326
00:09:25,150 --> 00:09:26,510
The pause failed...

327
00:09:26,890 --> 00:09:28,390
by a single vote.

328
00:09:28,930 --> 00:09:30,610
So who decides?

329
00:09:30,900 --> 00:09:32,140
Whatever the answer,

330
00:09:32,250 --> 00:09:34,480
the prize was never one mammoth.

331
00:09:34,980 --> 00:09:37,150
It is that child, grown up,

332
00:09:37,280 --> 00:09:38,710
in a world where elephants,

333
00:09:38,750 --> 00:09:40,110
ferrets, and thousands

334
00:09:40,150 --> 00:09:41,750
of others are still alive...

335
00:09:42,080 --> 00:09:43,500
not just on a poster.

336
00:09:44,020 --> 00:09:45,990
The mammoth is the poster.

337
00:09:46,250 --> 00:09:48,200
The freezer is the ark.

338
00:09:50,360 --> 00:09:51,360
These films exist

339
00:09:51,400 --> 00:09:52,320
so that the arrival

340
00:09:52,360 --> 00:09:53,670
of superintelligence goes

341
00:09:53,710 --> 00:09:54,970
well for everybody...

342
00:09:55,080 --> 00:09:55,990
and for everything

343
00:09:56,030 --> 00:09:57,280
that lives here with us.

344
00:09:57,630 --> 00:09:58,630
Want that too?

345
00:09:58,710 --> 00:09:59,710
Then subscribe.

